WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:52.480
Speaker 1: I got a voicemail that that&nbsp;
sort of plays on this idea, and it's harsh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:58.800
but I don't think it's inaccurate. Take a listen.
Speaker 3: Hey, David, it's an issue in Milwaukee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.760
I just had to contact you after listening to&nbsp;
your story about jury duty. First of all, I agree&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:11.840
the majority of people are really&nbsp;
not competent to serve on a jury.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:17.120
Why I say this is because the people you described&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:25.840
sound an awful lot like the people I work with&nbsp;
every day. They're great, nice people, but.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:36.160
The intelligence required. To think through a&nbsp;
guilty versus not guilty verdict and all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.160 --> 00:01:41.040
nuances of that. They just don't have it.
Speaker 1: Yeah, I mean, listen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:49.760
this is tough because I don't want to make fun&nbsp;
of people, but. The issue, what was at issue in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:57.680
the jury I served on was, was someone punching&nbsp;
someone else in the face? Self-Defense. And the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:02.800
judge made very clear there are rules this was&nbsp;
in Massachusetts at the time. There are rules for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:09.680
what counts as self-defense in the state and you&nbsp;
do have a duty to retreat. And if you could just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:15.840
turn around and walk away from the situation,&nbsp;
it's not self-defense. And in the jury room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.800
four of the six people, two just had no clue what&nbsp;
was going on, but two insisted. Now if someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:27.200
comes up to you and says something like this,&nbsp;
you punch him in the face. That's self-defense,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:32.480
man. That's self-defense. And it ended up&nbsp;
being like a six seven hour deliberation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.680
which shocked the judge. It shocked the lawyers.&nbsp;
It shocked everybody. And this was really,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.960
really simple. And so when we think about the&nbsp;
Kyle Rittenhouse trial and we listen to a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.440
the things that were said by potential jurors and&nbsp;
even people who made it onto the jury yesterday,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:52.400
I know that a lot of people like to say it's&nbsp;
not a perfect system, but it's the best system&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.680
we have. You're judged by a jury of your peers.&nbsp;
I'm not saying I have a better idea for how it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:04.800
should be done, but there's a lot of incompetent&nbsp;
people making what are for others life and death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:10.400
decisions, not literally in the sense of the death&nbsp;
penalty all the time, but going to prison for 10,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:15.360
20 years. That's a life and death decision in the&nbsp;
sense of it impacts your life for the rest of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:21.120
life. And I don't know any way to put it politely,&nbsp;
but it's a very, very scary situation we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

